{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-05-08T11:07:19.455Z","sessionId":"49549d0b-166a-48f3-a7cd-2a93ea53dbe7","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a7b9c75eb85272981</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-soph-Work-entire-devenv-cli-experiments/49549d0b-166a-48f3-a7cd-2a93ea53dbe7/tasks/a7b9c75eb85272981.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Security review of new auth library\" completed</summary>\n<result>Note: `stsClient.BaseURL = m.cfg.Issuer` and `Path = m.cfg.STSPath`. The core token is sent only as the body's `subject_token` — not URL/headers. Good.\n\nBut: the **core token is sent over HTTP** if `m.cfg.Issuer` is `http://`. Single-host setups guard via `requireSecureBaseURL` check at command entry. But the tokenmanager itself doesn't check scheme on `Issuer`. STS path goes wherever Issuer points. Worth noting — though in practice `NewAuthenticatedAPIClient` calls `api.RequireSecureURL(api.AuthBaseURL())` first. The library doesn't enforce HTTPS internally (consistent with \"library is config-driven\"). Fine.\n\nNow I'll deliver findings.\n\n---\n\n# Security Review: `alex/cli-auth-consolidation` auth library\n\nScope: `auth/{deviceflow,sts,tokens,tokenstore,tokenmanager,internal/oauthhttp}`, `cmd/entire/cli/auth/`, `cmd/entire/cli/api/auth_tokens.go`, `cmd/entire/cli/{api_client,login,logout}.go`, `cmd/entire/cli/dispatch/`.\n\nOverall posture: solid. The cache key correctly binds `(core, resource, audience, requested_token_type, scope)`; `coreTokenAudienceIncludes` is the only consumer of `tokens.ParseClaims` and is used only for routing (matches README); `DeleteCoreToken` ordering matches spec (keyring first, cache only on success) and is pinned by `TestDeleteCoreToken_PreservesCacheOnStoreFailure`; tokens never appear in logs or URLs; no `Bearer ` mangling. No critical or high findings.\n\n## Findings\n\n### Medium\n\n1. **No core-token expiry check before reuse** — `auth/tokenmanager/tokenmanager.go:229-234` and `:336-363`. The same-host shortcut, the JWT-aud shortcut, and the STS exchange all use the stored core token verbatim with no `tokens.Claims.ExpiresAt` check. A long-expired core token will be sent on every call and rejected with 401 by the resource (or, worse, returned by STS as an opaque error and surfaced as a confusing `token exchange: status 400: invalid_grant`). On a server that accepts an expired-but-undetected token (clock skew), it could even be exchanged for a fresh resource token. The core token's `exp` is already accessible via `tokens.ParseClaims`; either preflight-check it or surface a `re-login required` error rather than a generic STS failure. Not a confidentiality issue, but a real correctness/availability cliff for users who leave the CLI logged in for weeks.\n\n2. **`SaveCoreToken` does not clear the exchange cache** — `auth/tokenmanager/tokenmanager.go:140-142`. `DeleteCoreToken` clears it; `SaveCoreToken` does not. In practice the cache key includes `CoreToken`, so a new core can't return a stale exchanged token, but the old entries linger until process exit. If a future refactor were to drop `CoreToken` from the cache key (e.g., to share exchanges across re-logins for the same user), this becomes exploitable: a re-login under a different user could hand back the prior user's exchanged tokens. Either clear the cache on save, or add a comment-and-test pinning the invariant that `CoreToken` is the load-bearing isolator.\n\n3. **STS response `issued_token_type` is decoded but never validated against the request** — `auth/sts/sts.go:128`. RFC 8693 §2.2.1 requires a server response to include `issued_token_type`; the library accepts whatever the server returns, then the manager caches the result keyed by `RequestedTokenType` regardless. If a misconfigured server downgrades (e.g., issues an opaque access token when JWT was requested) the CLI silently uses the wrong shape. Low likelihood; spec-required validation.\n\n### Low\n\n4. **String-equality `Resource` comparison without normalisation** — `auth/tokenmanager/tokenmanager.go:229,232` (`m.cfg.Issuer == req.Resource`, `slices.Contains(claims.Audience, target)`). Trailing slash, case in scheme/host, default-port differences cause the same-host and aud-shortcuts to miss and force an exchange. Not a confidentiality issue but predictable footgun: a user rotating `ENTIRE_AUTH_BASE_URL=https://entire.io/` (trailing slash) will burn an STS round-trip every call. Normalise both sides through `url.Parse` + canonical form, or document strict `==` matching.\n\n5. **Auth-tokens path picker reads the env var directly** — `cmd/entire/cli/api/auth_tokens.go:32-42`. `authTokensProviderVersionEnvVar` duplicates `auth.ProviderVersionEnvVar`; the comment justifies it as avoiding an import cycle, but it means a future provider rename/typo in one constant won't break the other. If `ENTIRE_AUTH_PROVIDER_VERSION` ever drifts (e.g., a `v3` only the auth package knows about), `ListTokens`/`RevokeToken` will route to the v1 path while login goes to v3. Low risk today, but worth a single-source-of-truth helper in a leaf package both can import.\n\n6. **`readAPIErrorResponse` (deviceflow) dumps up to 1MB of HTML on non-200 with HTML body** — `auth/deviceflow/deviceflow.go:248-264`. Only the success path (`ReadAndDecodeJSON`) detects HTML and surfaces `ErrNonJSONResponse`. A captive portal returning 502 with an HTML page falls through to `text := strings.TrimSpace(string(body))` and the entire HTML appears in the error message. UX issue, not security, but consistency would be good — share the HTML detector.\n\n7. **`StartDeviceAuth` uses `DisallowUnknownFields` (strict=true); `PollDeviceAuth` does not** — `auth/deviceflow/deviceflow.go:130,177`. Asymmetric. A compromised/buggy AS that adds a field to `Start` fails the response, but the same field on `Poll` is silently ignored. Not exploitable today; pin both to strict for defence-in-depth, or document why poll is permissive.\n\n8. **Login retries on transient errors *include* `ErrNonJSONResponse`** — `cmd/entire/cli/login.go:118-130` reaches the transient branch if `PollDeviceAuth` returns any error that doesn't pass `oauthErrorParts`. An HTML-returning captive portal causes 5 polls before giving up (~5–25s wasted, plus repeated bursts of error to the user). Minor.\n\n### Nit\n\n9. **`auth/sts/sts.go:200`** — the body read in `readAPIError` swallows the read error with `//nolint:errcheck`. Best-effort is fine; document so future readers don't assume the body is complete.\n\n10. **`auth/tokenstore/keyring.go:104`** — encoded TokenSet JSON includes empty `expires_at` only via `omitempty` on the wire struct; `t.Scope` and `t.TokenType` likewise. Looks fine; nothing to fix.\n\n## Test coverage gaps that would let real regressions through\n\n- No test that a malformed/tampered core token JWT (signature won't be checked, but malformed payload) does **not** silently match the audience shortcut. *`TestCoreTokenAudienceShortcut_FallsThroughOnMalformedJWT`* covers the unparseable case but not \"well-formed JWT, attacker-supplied `aud` claim\" — though that's acceptable since unverified JWT was the explicit design choice and the resource will reject the token.\n- No test pins that `Resource` ends up in the STS form when the same-host/aud shortcuts are bypassed by an explicit `Audience`. *`TestToken_ExplicitAudienceBypassesAudienceShortcut`* checks `Audience` but not `Resource` in that path.\n- No test that `SaveCoreToken` on a manager with a populated cache yields fresh exchanges (related to finding 2).\n- No integration test that the tokenmanager refuses to send the core over `http://` (it doesn't — see review note above; intentional, but worth pinning).\n\nFiles referenced (absolute paths):\n- `/Users/soph/Work/entire/devenv/cli_experiments/auth/tokenmanager/tokenmanager.go`\n- `/Users/soph/Work/entire/devenv/cli_experiments/auth/sts/sts.go`\n- `/Users/soph/Work/entire/devenv/cli_experiments/auth/deviceflow/deviceflow.go`\n- `/Users/soph/Work/entire/devenv/cli_experiments/auth/tokenstore/keyring.go`\n- `/Users/soph/Work/entire/devenv/cli_experiments/auth/internal/oauthhttp/jsonresp.go`\n- `/Users/soph/Work/entire/devenv/cli_experiments/cmd/entire/cli/api/auth_tokens.go`\n- `/Users/soph/Work/entire/devenv/cli_experiments/cmd/entire/cli/login.go`</result>\n<usage><total_tokens>128355</total_tokens><tool_uses>42</tool_uses><duration_ms>218484</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
