{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-05-08T11:09:22.760Z","sessionId":"49549d0b-166a-48f3-a7cd-2a93ea53dbe7","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a0e37afb34d7dcb1e</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-soph-Work-entire-devenv-cli-experiments/49549d0b-166a-48f3-a7cd-2a93ea53dbe7/tasks/a0e37afb34d7dcb1e.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Critical correctness review\" completed</summary>\n<result>There's no review folder for this branch, and the user asked for findings inline. The user also said \"Do NOT Write report/summary/findings/analysis .md files. Return findings directly as your final assistant message.\"\n\n# Code Review — `alex/cli-auth-consolidation`\n\n## Critical\nNone.\n\n## High\n\n- **`SetManagerForTest` racy package var** — `cmd/entire/cli/auth/exchange.go:30,38`. `managerForTest` is a plain package var accessed without a mutex; the comment at line 30 calls out \"tests use SetManagerForTest\" but every test that touches it (`exchange_test.go:60`, `provider_test.go`) must remain non-parallel forever or the race detector will fire. The current test in `exchange_test.go:44` correctly notes \"Not parallel\", but as written the foot-gun is invisible to future contributors. Either guard with a `sync.Mutex`/`atomic.Pointer`, or have `SetManagerForTest` panic if called from a test that has already opted into `t.Parallel()` (no clean way), or document in the function godoc that callers must not run in parallel. The doc-only fix is fine, but this is one wrong `t.Parallel()` away from a flaky CI.\n\n- **`defaultManager` singleton vs env-driven config** — `cmd/entire/cli/auth/exchange.go:48,52`. `sync.Once` permanently pins `Issuer/STSPath/clientID` to whatever the first caller saw. Any non-test code path that triggers `TokenForResource` during package init or before `t.Setenv(\"ENTIRE_API_BASE_URL\", …)` runs in a test will cement the wrong issuer. The comment at exchange.go:46 acknowledges this but production has the same hazard during partial process upgrades (`exec.Command` chains, etc.). Consider exposing a `Reset()` for tests + a constructor that takes config explicitly. At minimum, log/panic if the second `defaultManager()` call observes a different `api.AuthBaseURL()` than the cached manager's `Issuer()`.\n\n- **Library `Issuer == Resource` comparison is byte-exact** — `auth/tokenmanager/tokenmanager.go:229`. If a third-party caller passes `https://entire.io` for `Issuer` and `https://entire.io/` for `Resource`, the same-host shortcut misses and STS fires unnecessarily — or worse, fails because `Config.STSPath` is empty (single-host expectation), surfacing `ErrNoSTSPath` instead of the core token. The cmd-side wraps this via `api.normalizeBaseURL`, but the library doc (`README.md`, `tokenmanager.go:208`) advertises this as a general-purpose library. Either normalize internally (parse + reconstruct origin) or document the contract loudly in the `Resource` field's godoc.\n\n- **Resource not echoed in cache key normalization** — `auth/tokenmanager/tokenmanager.go:301`. Same root cause as above: `cacheKey.Resource` stores the caller-supplied string, so `https://api.entire.io` and `https://api.entire.io/` cache as two entries. Not a correctness bug today but doubles the exchange count for sloppy callers.\n\n## Medium\n\n- **`tokenstore/keyring.go` decodes lenient JSON without trip-wire** — `auth/tokenstore/keyring.go:111-131`. Any well-formed JSON object (e.g. an unrelated CLI's blob the user happens to have keyed against the same `(service, profile)`) decodes to `keyringTokenSet{}` with a zero AccessToken, so it returns `tokens.TokenSet{}` with no error. The manager then sees a stored entry with empty access token and returns `ErrNotLoggedIn` rather than triggering the bare-string fallback. Add an explicit check after Unmarshal: if `wire.AccessToken == \"\"` AND no other recognised fields are set, surface `ErrMalformed` so the legacy fallback fires.\n\n- **`auth/tokens/tokens.go:121` `decodeAudience` masks underlying error** — when `aud` is a JSON object/number, both `Unmarshal` calls fail and the returned error reads `\"decode JWT aud claim: not a string or array\"` with no source-error context. Wrap one of the two attempt errors so `errors.Unwrap` still surfaces the cause.\n\n- **`auth/sts/sts.go:117` swallowed transport error** — `httpClient.Do` failure returns `fmt.Errorf(\"token exchange: %w\", err)`, but the caller (`tokenmanager.runExchange`) further wraps via the calling shim. `cmd/entire/cli/auth/exchange.go:79` already returns the lib error verbatim, so a single 30s outage propagates as `\"resolve API token: token exchange: Post …: dial tcp: i/o timeout\"`. Caller cannot distinguish \"logged out\" (`ErrNotLoggedIn`) from \"auth host unreachable\". Consider exporting `ErrSTSUnreachable` for net errors, or at minimum classifying `*url.Error.Timeout()`/`net.OpError` so `api_client.go:38-42` can show a different message than \"not logged in (run 'entire login' first)\".\n\n- **`tokenmanager.LookupCoreToken` collapses non-`ErrNotFound` store errors into wrapped errors** — `auth/tokenmanager/tokenmanager.go:147-156`. The test at `tokenmanager_test.go:652` asserts the contract that real keyring failures must surface non-`ErrNotLoggedIn`, which is good. But the cmd-side `auth.Store.LoadTokens` (`store.go:112-129`) only triggers the bare-string fallback on `ErrMalformed`, not on permission-denied errors. If keyring lookup fails with anything other than `ErrNotFound`/`ErrMalformed`, the user sees `\"resolve API token: load core token: get token from keyring: &lt;error&gt;\"`. That's correct error propagation but no path-to-recover guidance (\"run keyring unlock\", \"set ENTIRE_AUTH_BASE_URL\", etc.). Minor UX fit-and-finish.\n\n- **`PollDeviceAuth` body is read into memory before being checked for HTML** — `auth/internal/oauthhttp/jsonresp.go:39-46`. The 1 MB cap protects against huge bodies, but a slow-loris HTML response that drips bytes within `MaxResponseBytes` will block the whole poll loop until the read completes. The HTTP client has no per-request `Timeout`. Set a sensible default `c.HTTP.Timeout` in `deviceflow.Client` / `sts.Client` constructors, or use a `context.WithTimeout` inside the Exchange/Poll method bodies.\n\n- **`exchange.go:35` interface accepts only `Helper()`** — fine for tests, but `t.Cleanup` would be more idiomatic and would let `SetManagerForTest` register its own cleanup so callers can't forget the `defer cleanup()`. Tests already do `t.Cleanup(cleanup)`; promoting the cleanup registration into `SetManagerForTest` removes a foot-gun.\n\n## Low\n\n- **`oauthErrorParts` substring parsing is fragile** — `cmd/entire/cli/auth/client.go:122-133`. Detection of \"unknown OAuth code\" relies on `strings.CutPrefix(err.Error(), \"oauth error: \")`. Any future change in `deviceflow.errCodeToSentinel`'s format string silently breaks this (no compile-time link). Consider exporting an `ErrUnknownOAuthCode` sentinel or a typed error from `deviceflow` so the cmd-side matches via `errors.As`.\n\n- **Tests that rely on `t.Setenv` near package init** — `cmd/entire/cli/api/auth_tokens_test.go:13,74,145` and `provider_test.go`. If `auth_tokens.go` ever caches the env-var value (e.g. behind `sync.Once`), these tests would silently start sharing state. The env-driven path already has this hazard in `exchange.go`. Add a `// REGRESSION:` style comment if you're depending on the lazy read.\n\n- **`auth/sts/sts.go:200` body read on error path drops the read error** — `body, _ := io.ReadAll(...)`. If the response body errors mid-read, the error message becomes `\"token exchange: status NNN\"` with no body context. Probably fine, but a `body, readErr := …; if readErr != nil { return fmt.Errorf(\"…: read body: %w\", readErr) }` would be a one-line improvement.\n\n- **No test for STS `slow_down` followed by transport error** — coverage gap. The polling loop's transient-counter reset (`login.go:132`) is tested, but the interaction of slow_down → transport error → slow_down isn't pinned. Low risk.\n\n- **Cache never bounded** — `auth/tokenmanager/tokenmanager.go:117`. CLI processes are short-lived so unbounded growth is fine, but if a long-running daemon ever embeds this library, the cache map grows monotonically (entries only deleted on expiry-miss). Note in godoc that the manager is intended for short-lived processes, or add a TTL-based reaper.\n\n## Praise\n\n- The split into `deviceflow`/`sts`/`tokens`/`tokenstore`/`tokenmanager` is clean and the no-globals discipline (every URL/path injected via `Config`) reads well. The `README.md` design-principles section is unusually grown-up for an internal lib.\n- The struct-keyed cache (`cacheKey`) is the right call over delimiter-joined strings; the comment at `tokenmanager.go:285` documents *why*, which prevents the inevitable \"let's just use `fmt.Sprintf`\" regression.\n- Tests around delete-then-relogin (`TestDeleteCoreToken_ClearsExchangeCache`, `TestDeleteCoreToken_PreservesCacheOnStoreFailure`) are excellent — they pin the contract that's easy to break in a refactor.\n- `TestCoreTokenAudienceShortcut_FallsThroughOnMalformedJWT` correctly identifies and tests the security-sensitive fallthrough; the test's comment is the kind of thing future readers will thank you for.\n- The shadow `ErrNonJSONResponse` path with a captive-portal-friendly message is genuinely helpful UX.\n- The legacy bare-string fallback is well-tested and the comment in `store.go:103-111` clearly states the upgrade path. The `ErrMalformed` sentinel boundary is the right design choice.\n- The PR-review-fix commits (parallel-safe clock pin, struct cache key, PollDeviceAuth retry, etc.) show responsive iteration with clear commit messages.\n\n## Summary\n\n**Verdict: APPROVE WITH MINOR FOLLOW-UPS.** No correctness blockers; the split-host STS and resource-routing fixes look sound and the test coverage is good. The primary risks (`SetManagerForTest` racy var, singleton cementing config, byte-exact URL compare) are foot-guns rather than bugs in current call sites — they should be cleaned up before another CLI adopts the `auth/` library, but they don't block this consolidation. Estimated effort: ~half a day to address High items, another half-day for Medium.</result>\n<usage><total_tokens>182793</total_tokens><tool_uses>57</tool_uses><duration_ms>318974</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
