:39-46`. The 1 MB cap protects against huge bodies, but a slow-loris HTML response that drips bytes within `MaxResponseBytes` will block the whole poll loop until the read completes. The HTTP client has no per-request `Timeout`. Set a sensible default `c.HTTP.Timeout` in `deviceflow.Client` / `sts.Client` constructors, or use a `context.WithTimeout` inside the Exchange/Poll method bodies.\n\n- **`exchange.go:35` interface accepts only `Helper()`** — fine for tests, but `t.Cleanup` would be more idiomatic and would let `SetManagerForTest` register its own cleanup so callers can't forget the `defer cleanup()`. Tests already do `t.Cleanup(cleanup)`; promoting the cleanup registration into `SetManagerForTest` removes a foot-gun.\n\n## Low\n\n- **`oauthErrorParts` substring parsing is fragile** — `cmd/entire/cli/auth/client.go:122-133`. Detection of \"unknown OAuth code\" relies on `strings.CutPrefix(err.Error(), \"oauth error: \")`. Any future change in `deviceflow.errCodeToSentinel`'s format string silently breaks this (no compile-time link). Consider exporting an `ErrUnknownOAuthCode` sentinel or a typed error from `deviceflow` so the cmd-side matches via `errors.As`.\n\n- **Tests that rely on `t.Setenv` near package init** — `cmd/entire/cli/api/auth_tokens_test.go:13,74,145` and `provider_test.go`. If `auth_tokens.go` ever caches the env-var value (e.g. behind `sync.Once`), these tests would silently start sharing state. The env-driven path already has this hazard in `exchange.go`. Add a `// REGRESSION:` style comment if you're depending on the lazy read.\n\n- **`auth/sts/sts.go:200` body read on error path drops the read error** — `body, _ := io.ReadAll(...)`. If the response body errors mid-read, the error message becomes `\"token exchange: status NNN\"` with no body context. Probably fine, but a `body, readErr := …; if readErr != nil { return fmt.Errorf(\"…: read body: %w\", readErr) }` would be a one-line improvement.\n\n- **No test for STS `slow_down` followed by transport error** — coverage gap. The polling loop's transient-counter reset (`login.go:132`) is tested, but the interaction of slow_down → transport error → slow_down isn't pinned. Low risk.\n\n- **Cache never bounded** — `auth/tokenmanager/tokenmanager.go:117`. CLI processes are short-lived so unbounded growth is fine, but if a long-running daemon ever embeds this library, the cache map grows monotonically (entries only deleted on expiry-miss). Note in godoc that the manager is intended for short-lived processes, or add a TTL-based reaper.\n\n## Praise\n\n- The split into `deviceflow`/`sts`/`tokens`/`tokenstore`/`tokenmanager` is clean and the no-globals discipline (every URL/path injected via `Config`) reads well. The `README.md` design-principles section is unusually grown-up for an internal lib.\n- The struct-keyed cache (`cacheKey`) is the right call over delimiter-joined strings; the comment at `tokenmanager.go:285` documents *why*, which prevents the inevitable \"let's just use `fmt.Sprintf`\" regression.\n- Tests around delete-then-relogin (`TestDeleteCoreToken_ClearsExchangeCache`, `TestDeleteCoreToken_PreservesCacheOnStoreFailure`) are excellent — they pin the contract that's easy to break in a refactor.\n- `TestCoreTokenAudienceShortcut_FallsThroughOnMalformedJWT` correctly identifies and tests the security-sensitive fallthrough; the test's comment is the kind of thing future readers will thank you for.\n- The shadow `ErrNonJSONResponse` path with a captive-portal-friendly message is genuinely helpful UX.\n- The legacy bare-string fallback is well-tested and the comment in `store.go:103-111` clearly states the upgrade path. The `ErrMalformed` sentinel boundary is the right design choice.\n- The PR-review-fix commits (parallel-safe clock pin, struct cache key, PollDeviceAuth retry, etc.) show responsive iteration with clear commit messages.\n\n## Summary\n\n**Verdict: APPROVE WITH MINOR FOLLOW-UPS.** No correctness blockers; the split-host STS and resource-routing fixes look sound and the test coverage is good. The primary risks (`SetManagerForTest` racy var, singleton cementing config, byte-exact URL compare) are foot-guns rather than bugs in current call sites — they should be cleaned up before another CLI adopts the `auth/` library, but they don't block this consolidation. Estimated effort: ~half a day to address High items, another half-day for Medium.</result>\n<usage><total_tokens>182793</total_tokens><tool_uses>57</tool_uses><duration_ms>318974</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"},"uuid":"1a64735e-40f7-4a35-bca0-04f4e4649b7c","timestamp":"2026-05-08T11:09:22.772Z","permissionMode":"default","origin":{"kind":"task-notification"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/soph/Work/entire/devenv/cli_experiments","sessionId":"49549d0b-166a-48f3-a7cd-2a93ea53dbe7","version":"2.1.129","gitBranch":"alex/cli-auth-consolidation"}
